# Registering the grader MCP in mcphub (2026-10-07)

Companion to `grading-surface-v1`. That memo built `agent/gate_grader.py` as a stdio
server. This records what the NAS side actually requires, and why stdio alone is not
enough — findings from nas-goose, written to
`/volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/data-sourcing-agent/MCPHUB-GRADEMCP-REGISTRATION-20261006.md`.

## mcphub cannot host a stdio server

mcphub spawns `stdio` servers as **child processes inside its own container**
(`serverConfigValidation.js`: "spawn `command`/`args` locally as the MCPHub
process"; `mcpService.js` uses `new StdioClientTransport({command, args})`). It has
**no docker CLI and no docker socket**, so it cannot `docker exec` into our
container either. A `python -m agent.gate_grader` stdio entry would run where the
`agent` package and `/state` do not exist.

So the grading MCP is exposed over **streamable-http** instead, and mcphub
registers the **URL**. This is why `gate-grader` grew `--transport streamable-http`
(the server is identical; only the wire changes) and why compose gained a
`gate-grader` service on `ai_proxy` at port **8802** (no host port — only peers need
it).

## The registration, once the image is deployed

mcphub's source of truth is **PostgreSQL**, not `mcp_settings.json` (that JSON is
imported once on first start only) — so changes go through the **API**, and the
live `data` group id is `2c646542-70e2-4f6f-9664-962885b20006` (the id in the JSON
is stale). Auth: `POST /api/auth/login` → token, then header `x-auth-token`.

```bash
cd /volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/mcphub
set -a; . ./..env; set +a
DATA_GROUP=2c646542-70e2-4f6f-9664-962885b20006
TOK=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "{\"username\":\"admin\",\"password\":\"$MCPHUB_ADMIN_PASSWORD\"}" \
  | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["token"])')

# 1) create the server
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/servers \
  -H "x-auth-token: $TOK" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"name":"gate-grader","config":{
        "type":"streamable-http",
        "url":"http://data-sourcing-agent-gate-grader:8802/mcp",
        "description":"Gate grading: next_request, queue, record_grade, status, grades",
        "enabled":true,"owner":"admin"}}'

# 2) add it to the live 'data' group
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8781/api/groups/$DATA_GROUP/servers \
  -H "x-auth-token: $TOK" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"serverName":"gate-grader"}'
```

Group entry: `{"name":"gate-grader","tools":"all","prompts":"all","resources":"all"}`.
Rollback: `DELETE /api/groups/$DATA_GROUP/servers/gate-grader` then
`DELETE /api/servers/gate-grader`.

> Naming note: it first deployed under the generic name `grademcp`, which said it
> grades *something* but not what. Renamed to **`gate-grader`** on 2026-10-07 —
> the name the server already gave itself — across the module, the compose service,
> and the mcphub registration.

## How it rolled out (done, 2026-10-07)

The image publish step (`.buildkite/pipeline.yml`) is `if: build.branch == "main"`,
so the deployed NAS image (`main`, revision `9ad505f`) had **no `gate-grader.py` or
`grader.py`** — those lived only on `feat/jev-gate`. The sequence that landed it:

1. **Merged PR #13** (`feat/jev-gate` → `main`, merge commit `aefaa5ae`). Buildkite
   build #42 passed and published `:latest` and `:aefaa5ae` to GHCR.
2. **Synced the NAS compose**
   (`/volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/data-sourcing-agent/compose.yaml`, formerly `app`
   only) with the repo's `grader` + `gate-grader` services (backup kept as
   `compose.yaml.bak-pr13-20261006-225544`), then `docker compose up -d`.
3. **Registered** as above.

Verified state: image `:latest` = `:aefaa5ae` = `8a685596dbc1`; container
`data-sourcing-agent-gate-grader` **running/healthy** on `ai_proxy` (`172.21.0.37`);
`POST http://data-sourcing-agent-gate-grader:8802/mcp` from the mcphub container →
**HTTP 200**; `GET /api/servers/gate-grader` → `status: connected` (tools
`next_request`, `queue`, `record_grade`, `status`, `grades`); the `data` group is
now `[databento-discovery, memos, gate-grader]`. `GATE_ENFORCE` is still blank, so the
gate deployed observe-only — as intended. Rollout log:
`/volumeUSB1/usbshare/docker/data-sourcing-agent/ROLLOUT-PROGRESS-20261006.md`.
